<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dadusha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 1800 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dadusha was a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a> in the Diyala region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> in the early 18th century BCE, the brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a> and son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ipiq-adad-ii">Ipiq-Adad II</a>. He made Eshnunna one of the great powers of the region alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i">Shamshi-Adad I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Upper Mesopotamia</a>. His victory stele, now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-museum-of-iraq">Iraq Museum</a>, records a campaign against the city of Qabara.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dadusha.jpg" alt="Dadusha" width="320" height="479" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stele_of_Dadusha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
